# The seat daemon, without which a compositor cannot open the DRM device.
#
# WHY IT IS A SERVICE AND NOT A LIBRARY CALL. wlroots' session backend needs
# libseat AND a running seatd: backend/session/meson.build:11 refuses to build
# session support without `udev.found() and libseat.found()`, and libseat's
# default backend then connects to a daemon on /run/seatd.sock. The package was
# built and installed -- /usr/bin/seatd and /usr/bin/seatd-launch are both in
# its [installs] listing -- and NOTHING STARTED IT. So labwc would have failed
# to acquire a session even once it was launched, and the error would have
# named DRM rather than seatd.
#
# type = process BECAUSE IT MUST KEEP RUNNING. This is the dinit lesson from
# the two-VM DHCP test: a daemon whose job is to stay up cannot be modelled as
# a step that completes. seatd holds the socket for the life of the session.
#
# -g video PUTS THE SOCKET IN A GROUP RATHER THAN LEAVING IT ROOT-ONLY. Today
# every process here runs as root and it makes no difference; when there is a
# real user, this is the line that lets them run a compositor without being
# root.
#
# AND THE GROUP HAD TO EXIST, WHICH IT DID NOT. This service named `video`
# while /etc/group held only root, tty, nogroup and _dhcpcd, so seatd could
# not resolve it and exited:
#
#     dinit: Service seatd process terminated with exit code 1
#     dinit: Service seatd restarting too quickly; stopping.
#
# labwc then started, found no seat, and exited 1 in turn -- so the whole
# desktop failed on a group name. video:x:39 is now in /etc/group; 39 is the
# conventional GID and any free number would have done.
#
# The first screenshot run is what surfaced this: the frame was black and the
# session console said exactly why, which is the argument for capturing the
# console alongside the image rather than the image alone.
#
# depends-on early-filesystems BECAUSE /run MUST BE A tmpfs FIRST. The image
# root is read-only; guest/init mounts /run before exec'ing dinit and the
# socket goes there.
type = process
command = /usr/bin/seatd -g video -n 3

# ready-notification, BECAUSE depends-on IS NOT ENOUGH FOR type = process.
# dinit calls a process service STARTED the moment it forks, so labwc ran while
# seatd was still initialising and labwc-session's own guard caught it:
#
#     labwc-session: no /run/seatd.sock -- seatd is not running
#
# every boot since the desktop existed. The socket was there a second later,
# which is why starting labwc by hand always worked and why this looked like
# nothing was wrong.
#
# seatd SIGNALS AT THE RIGHT MOMENT, which is the reason this is the fix rather
# than a sleep. seatd.c:182-201 opens the socket, binds, listens, chmods and
# chowns it, adds it to the poller, and only then writes to the fd given by -n.
# `-n <fd>` is documented in its own usage text. dinit holds the service in
# STARTING until that write arrives, so depends-on = seatd now means what it
# reads like.
ready-notification = pipefd:3
restart = true
depends-on = early-filesystems
